\description{
Fits a model with a \code{breakpt()} term (see the threshold models section of
\code{\link[=sdmTMB]{sdmTMB()}}) over a grid of fixed cutpoints, finds the cutpoint with the
lowest negative log likelihood (NLL), and optionally refits the model with
the cutpoint fixed at that value. Use this in place of estimating the
cutpoint directly with \code{\link[=sdmTMB]{sdmTMB()}}.
}
\details{
The NLL of a \code{breakpt()} model has a kink in the cutpoint at every
observation, so an optimizer that estimates the cutpoint with the other
parameters can stop at the wrong cutpoint while reporting convergence, and
its standard error for the cutpoint isn't reliable. With the cutpoint held
fixed, the likelihood is smooth in the other parameters.

The profile works in three steps:
\enumerate{
\item Fit the model at each cutpoint in \code{cuts}, working outward from the median
of the covariate.
\item Wherever the profile is within \code{within} NLL units of its best, add
cutpoints until neighbouring cutpoints are at most \code{tol_coarse} apart.
Next to the best cutpoint, and on either side of each point where the
profile crosses the interval cutoff, continue until they are at most
\code{tol} apart.
\item Search for the minimum between the best cutpoint's two neighbours with
\code{\link[stats:optimize]{stats::optimize()}}.
}

Each profile fit starts from the nearest finished fit and skips the extra
optimization steps and \code{\link[RTMB:sdreport]{RTMB::sdreport()}} for speed.
Only the final fit runs those.

The confidence interval runs from the lowest to the highest cutpoint within
\code{qchisq(level, 1) / 2} NLL units of the best, with the ends interpolated
between grid points. If the profile rises above the cutoff between them
(\code{estimate$pieces} > 1), the interval includes those cutpoints too. If the
profile is still inside the cutoff at the end of the grid, that end is \code{NA}.

The interval is approximate: it treats twice the NLL difference as
chi-squared with 1 degree of freedom. That's reasonable when the slope is
clearly non-zero. As the slope approaches zero, the cutpoint stops being
identified, the profile flattens, and the interval opens up to the ends of
the grid (see Warnings).
}
\section{Warnings}{

The function warns, and sets \code{estimate$open_interval}, \code{estimate$few_below},
or \code{estimate$few_above}, when the interval reaches the end of the grid or when
fewer than 15 observations (or 5\%, if that's more) fall below or above the
best cutpoint. Either means the data say little about the cutpoint. Also check
\code{estimate$pieces} (the interval isn't continuous if greater than 1),
\code{estimate$near_edge}, \code{estimate$refine_complete}, and
\code{estimate$max_grad_near} (the largest gradient among fits within \code{within}
NLL units of the best).

Delta models, models with more than one threshold term, and a
\code{threshold_breakpt_cut} prior (see \code{\link[=sdmTMBpriors]{sdmTMBpriors()}}) aren't supported.
}

